      "diveDeep": "This question tests knowledge of Latin American authoritarianism and human rights principles. Choice 0 is incorrect and actually a fair legal procedure; the regimes didn't conduct fair trials. Choice 1 is incorrect—there's no mention of factories; the violence was targeted at people, not infrastructure. Choice 2 is technically what the regimes claimed ('they were arresting criminals and subversives'), but it's incorrect because these governments disappeared political opponents without trial, which is not lawful arrest. The UDHR prohibits arbitrary detention, enforced disappearance, and extrajudicial killing. Chile's military coup (1973) and Argentina's military rule (1976-83) systematically arrested, tortured, and killed political opponents—thousands died. Victims weren't tried; they vanished. The 'Mothers of the Plaza de Mayo' were families searching for disappeared relatives. This was state terrorism against civilians. On the Regents, when human rights violations are questioned, the key is distinguishing between lawful punishment (after trial) and unlawful repression (disappearance, torture, execution without trial). The UDHR protects life, liberty, due process, and prohibits torture.",
