      "diveDeep": "This question requires understanding both Putin's policies and comparisons to historical leaders. Choice 0 is incorrect—Nicholas II (last tsar, d. 1918) is too distant and was actually weak; Putin is assertive, not weak like Nicholas. Choice 2 is incorrect—Gorbachev ended the Cold War and reduced Soviet power; Putin opposes that legacy. Choice 3 is incorrect—Yeltsin was weak and allowed Russian power to decline (1990s); Putin opposed that trajectory. Stalin (1922-1953) expanded Soviet territory (Poland, Baltics, Eastern Europe), suppressed dissent, and made the USSR a superpower through force. Putin similarly: annexed Crimea (2014), intervened in Georgia and Syria, reasserted Russian influence, and suppressed domestic opposition. The cartoonist is drawing a direct analogy: Putin is reviving the aggressive expansionism and autocratic methods of Stalin to restore Soviet-era power. This comparison is controversial in Russia—Putin's supporters deny it; opponents embrace it as accurate. On the Regents, cartoon comparisons often draw parallels between leaders in different eras—identify what they share (expansionism, authoritarianism, ambition for power) rather than time period.",
